{%- doc -%}
  Required acknowledgement for products that are final-sale (made to order, embroidered,
  personalized). The block:

  - Renders a merchant-editable terms summary, then a required checkbox bound to the product
    form via `form="..."`, so native HTML5 submit-time validation blocks Add to Cart until
    ticked. The terms are wired to the checkbox with `aria-describedby`: the label is short
    ("I agree to the above") and does not carry the meaning on its own, so the terms are the
    accessible description rather than decoration.
  - Records the acknowledgement as a `properties[Return policy acknowledged]` line-item
    property, whose value names the size that was on screen when the shopper agreed. For
    support-ticket triage. Not a click-wrap contract.
  - Unticks itself whenever the shopper changes any option, which is what keeps that value
    honest: the box can only ever be ticked for the currently selected variant. Unticking
    re-hides accelerated checkout and is announced politely, because silently clearing a box
    the shopper ticked would otherwise just look like a broken Add to Cart button.
  - Hides the accelerated-checkout container (Shop Pay / Apple Pay / Google Pay) while the box
    is unchecked via a `data-return-policy-pending` attribute set on this element. The
    cross-block CSS hook is documented inline in `blocks/accelerated-checkout.liquid`.
  - Renders its validation message as visible `role="alert"` text, with `aria-invalid` on the
    checkbox and the message's id appended to its `aria-describedby`, rather than relying on the
    browser's native bubble. Native bubbles auto-dismiss, vanish on scroll or blur, cannot be
    magnified, and expose no programmatic association, so they cannot be the only error mechanism
    (WCAG 3.3.1). The bubble still fires; it is a bonus.

  **Blank `terms` hides the whole block.** A required checkbox reading "I agree to the above"
  with nothing above it is a consent gate with no terms, which is worse than no gate at all.

  That is why the schema declares `"tag": null` and this file emits its own
  `<return-policy-acknowledgment-component>` root: a schema-generated tag renders even when the
  block body is empty, and the fail-closed `:has()` rule would match that empty element and hide
  accelerated checkout with no checkbox present to ever un-hide it. Do not move the element back
  into the schema's `tag`.

  This block depends on a sibling `buy-buttons` block in the same section: the
  `product_form_id` is derived from the section id (`BuyButtons-ProductForm-{section.id}`)
  and the `<product-form-component>` element is queried within the section root by JS.

  Add the block to any product template via the theme editor. The block has no template
  restriction in its schema (theme-check rejects `enabled_on` on blocks); when added outside
  a product context the JS no-ops and logs a diagnostic warning to the console.
{%- enddoc -%}

{%- liquid
  assign block_settings = block.settings
  assign product_form_id = 'BuyButtons-ProductForm-' | append: section.id
  assign element_id = 'ReturnPolicyAck-' | append: block.id
  assign terms_id = 'ReturnPolicyAckTerms-' | append: block.id
  assign error_id = 'ReturnPolicyAckError-' | append: block.id
  assign label_text = block_settings.checkbox_label
  assign terms = block_settings.terms

  assign product_resource = closest.product

  comment
    The size option's position is resolved once here and handed to JS as a data attribute. The
    lookup itself is shared with the size-guide link via a global setting, because a theme block
    cannot read another block's settings. See snippets/size-option-position.liquid.
  endcomment
  capture size_position_raw
    render 'size-option-position', product_resource: product_resource
  endcapture
  assign size_position = size_position_raw | strip

  comment
    Server-rendered so the value is correct with JS off: without JS the variant picker's radios
    never rewrite the variant id, so the initially selected variant is the one that submits.
  endcomment
  assign selected_variant = product_resource.selected_or_first_available_variant
  if size_position != blank
    assign size_index = size_position | minus: 1
    assign selected_size = selected_variant.options[size_index]
  endif

  if selected_size != blank
    assign initial_value = 'products.product.return_policy_acknowledgment_value' | t: size: selected_size
  else
    assign initial_value = 'products.product.return_policy_acknowledgment_value_no_size' | t
  endif
-%}

{%- comment -%}
  Blank terms renders nothing at all, which is why this block declares `"tag": null` and emits
  its own custom element. With a schema-generated tag the wrapper renders even when the block
  body is empty, and the fail-closed `:has()` rule below would then match a component that has
  no checkbox in it: accelerated checkout hidden forever, with no gate the shopper could ever
  satisfy. No element, no gate.

  The data attributes belong on this element specifically, not an inner wrapper: the JS reads
  them off `this.dataset`, where `this` is the custom element.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if terms != blank -%}
  <return-policy-acknowledgment-component
    class="spacing-style"
    style="{% render 'spacing-style', settings: block_settings %}"
    data-product-id="{{ product_resource.id }}"
    data-size-option-position="{{ size_position }}"
    {{ block.shopify_attributes }}
  >
    {%- comment -%}
      The setting value must be the only child of its parent element, with no surrounding Liquid
      and no filter but `escape`, or the theme editor cannot live-update it. The {% if %} above
      is deliberately outside this div for the same reason, and no filter is applied because
      `escape` would render the rich text as visible markup.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div id="{{ terms_id }}" class="return-policy-acknowledgment__terms">{{ block.settings.terms }}</div>

    {% render 'checkbox',
      name: 'properties[Return policy acknowledged]',
      value: initial_value,
      label: label_text,
      id: element_id,
      checked: false,
      events: 'on:change="/handleChange"',
      disabled: false,
      required: true,
      formId: product_form_id,
      inputRef: 'checkbox',
      describedBy: terms_id
    %}

    {%- comment -%}
      The visible error message. Empty until JS fills it, so it never occupies space or announces
      on a valid form. `role="alert"` is what makes it announce the moment it is filled; without
      it the message appears silently unless focus happens to be on the checkbox. Matches
      blocks/embroidery-custom-text.liquid and the Form pattern in docs/accessibility-patterns.md.

      The JS pairs this with `aria-invalid` on the checkbox and appends this id to the checkbox's
      `aria-describedby` (not `aria-errormessage`, which has thinner support). That is why the
      shared checkbox snippet's `describedBy` param takes a token list.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <p
      id="{{ error_id }}"
      class="return-policy-acknowledgment__error"
      ref="errorMessage"
      role="alert"
      hidden
    ></p>

    <p class="return-policy-acknowledgment__policy-link">
      <a href="/policies/refund-policy">{{ 'products.product.return_policy_link_label' | t | escape }}</a>
    </p>

    {%- comment -%}
      Announces the automatic untick on option change. Politely: the shopper initiated an option
      change, not this, so it must not interrupt. Empty on load so it announces nothing until
      there is something to say.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      `role="status"` alone: it already implies aria-live="polite" plus aria-atomic="true", and
      carrying both has been reported to double-announce in some AT pairings.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <p
      class="return-policy-acknowledgment__status"
      role="status"
      ref="statusMessage"
    ></p>

    {%- comment -%}
      Translated string sources for JS. Permanently hidden; the JS reads textContent and, for
      the value template, substitutes the [size] placeholder. Mirrors the interpolation
      convention in snippets/localization-form.liquid.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <span hidden ref="invalidMessage">
      {{- 'products.product.return_policy_acknowledgment_invalid' | t | escape -}}
    </span>
    <span hidden ref="clearedMessage">
      {{- 'products.product.return_policy_acknowledgment_cleared' | t | escape -}}
    </span>
    <span hidden ref="valueTemplate">
      {{- 'products.product.return_policy_acknowledgment_value' | t: size: '[size]' | escape -}}
    </span>
    <span hidden ref="valueTemplateNoSize">
      {{- 'products.product.return_policy_acknowledgment_value_no_size' | t | escape -}}
    </span>

    <noscript>
      <style>
        product-form-component [ref="acceleratedCheckoutButtonContainer"] { display: none; }
      </style>
    </noscript>
  </return-policy-acknowledgment-component>
{%- endif -%}

{% stylesheet %}
  return-policy-acknowledgment-component {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
  }

  return-policy-acknowledgment-component .return-policy-acknowledgment__terms {
    margin-block-end: var(--padding-xs);
    font-size: var(--font-size--xs);
  }

  return-policy-acknowledgment-component .return-policy-acknowledgment__terms ul {
    margin-block: 0;
    padding-inline-start: 1.25em;
  }

  return-policy-acknowledgment-component .return-policy-acknowledgment__policy-link {
    margin-block: var(--padding-xs) 0;
    font-size: var(--font-size--xs);
  }

  return-policy-acknowledgment-component .return-policy-acknowledgment__error {
    margin-block: var(--padding-xs) 0;
    font-size: var(--font-size--xs);
    color: var(--color-error, currentcolor);
    font-weight: bold;
  }

  return-policy-acknowledgment-component .return-policy-acknowledgment__error[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }

  /*
   * The status region is announced, not seen. It must stay in the accessibility tree, so it
   * cannot use display:none or the visually-hidden clip trick would also drop it from some
   * screen readers' virtual buffers. This keeps it rendered but out of the layout.
   */
  return-policy-acknowledgment-component .return-policy-acknowledgment__status {
    position: absolute;
    width: 1px;
    height: 1px;
    margin: -1px;
    padding: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
    clip-path: inset(50%);
    white-space: nowrap;
    border: 0;
  }

  /*
   * The shared checkbox snippet (snippets/checkbox.liquid) ships with
   * white-space: nowrap + text-overflow: ellipsis on .checkbox__label-text
   * (assets/base.css:2634-2639) because it was designed for short labels
   * (e.g. "Include gift wrap"). The default label is short now, but the
   * setting is merchant-editable, so keep the wrap override.
   */
  return-policy-acknowledgment-component .checkbox__label {
    align-items: flex-start;
    line-height: 1.4;
  }

  return-policy-acknowledgment-component .checkbox__label-text {
    white-space: normal;
    overflow: visible;
    text-overflow: clip;
  }

  /*
   * Cross-block gate. Fail-closed: the selector matches whenever the ack
   * component does NOT have data-return-policy-pending='false', which covers
   * (a) the initial state before JS sets the attribute on connectedCallback
   * and (b) the unchecked state once JS is running. The :has() scope also
   * keeps the gate local to the section that contains the ack block.
   *
   * Cross-block dependency: targets the accelerated-checkout container by
   * its `ref` attribute (see blocks/accelerated-checkout.liquid). Renaming
   * the ref will silently disable the gate.
   *
   * The sticky add-to-cart bar is NOT hidden here. Instead the JS
   * intercepts its click in capture phase, scrolls the checkbox into view,
   * and calls reportValidity() to force the native bubble. See
   * assets/return-policy-acknowledgment.js#handleStickyClick.
   */
  .shopify-section:has(
    return-policy-acknowledgment-component:not([data-return-policy-pending='false'])
  ) [ref='acceleratedCheckoutButtonContainer'] {
    display: none;
  }
{% endstylesheet %}

<script
  src="{{ 'return-policy-acknowledgment.js' | asset_url }}"
  type="module"
  fetchpriority="low"
></script>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "t:names.return_policy_acknowledgment",
  "tag": null,
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "richtext",
      "id": "terms",
      "label": "t:settings.return_policy_acknowledgment.terms",
      "default": "t:settings.return_policy_acknowledgment.default_terms",
      "info": "t:settings.return_policy_acknowledgment.terms_info"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "checkbox_label",
      "label": "t:settings.return_policy_acknowledgment.label",
      "default": "t:settings.return_policy_acknowledgment.default_label",
      "info": "t:settings.return_policy_acknowledgment.label_info"
    },
    { "type": "header", "content": "t:content.padding" },
    { "type": "range", "id": "padding-block-start", "label": "t:settings.top", "min": 0, "max": 100, "step": 1, "unit": "px", "default": 0 },
    { "type": "range", "id": "padding-block-end", "label": "t:settings.bottom", "min": 0, "max": 100, "step": 1, "unit": "px", "default": 0 },
    { "type": "range", "id": "padding-inline-start", "label": "t:settings.left", "min": 0, "max": 100, "step": 1, "unit": "px", "default": 0 },
    { "type": "range", "id": "padding-inline-end", "label": "t:settings.right", "min": 0, "max": 100, "step": 1, "unit": "px", "default": 0 }
  ],
  "presets": [
    { "name": "t:names.return_policy_acknowledgment", "category": "t:categories.product" }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
